(window.PSAC_DEMO_DECKS = window.PSAC_DEMO_DECKS || {})[5] = {"grade":5,"packs":{"grade5-english":{"eng-nouns":[{"id":"g5eng-core-proper-02","q":"In “Mrs Wong teaches science at Phoenix Government School,” which is a common noun?","opts":["Phoenix Government School","Mrs Wong","Phoenix","science"],"a":"science","hint":"A common noun names a general subject, thing or person.","exp":"<b>Science</b> is a general subject name. The person, town and school names are proper nouns."},{"id":"g5eng-n-052","q":"Which sentence uses a possessive noun correctly for one dog?","opts":["The dogs bones is here.","The dogs' bone is here.","The dogs bone is here.","The dog's bone is here."],"a":"The dog's bone is here.","hint":"A possessive noun for a single owner takes apostrophe + s.","exp":"\"<b>The dog's bone</b>\" is correct - apostrophe + s shows the bone belongs to one dog. \"The dogs' bone\" would mean the bone belongs to several dogs."},{"id":"g5eng-meaning-abstract-8","q":"Nadia believed that her friend would return the borrowed book. Which abstract noun describes that belief in someone?","opts":["bag","trust","book","shelf"],"a":"trust","hint":"She believes her friend is reliable.","exp":"Trust means believing that someone is reliable or honest."},{"id":"g5eng-n-060","q":"In the sentence \"Her honesty earned her everyone's respect,\" which TWO words are abstract nouns?","opts":["earned and everyone's","respect and Her","Her and everyone's","honesty and respect"],"a":"honesty and respect","hint":"Abstract nouns name qualities or concepts that cannot be physically touched.","exp":"<b>Honesty</b> (a quality) and <b>respect</b> (a concept/feeling) are both abstract nouns. \"Her\" and \"everyone's\" are pronouns; \"earned\" is a verb."},{"id":"g5eng-syl-019","q":"Which country name needs <b>the</b> in front of it?","opts":["India","United States","Mauritius","Madagascar"],"a":"United States","hint":"One of these is a group of states rather than a single place.","exp":"We say <b>the United States</b>, because the name describes a group of states. Mauritius, India and Madagascar take no article."}],"eng-verbs":[{"id":"g5eng-core-voice-14","q":"Change to active: “Fresh bread is sold here every morning.”","opts":["Fresh bread sells them every morning.","They sold fresh bread here yesterday.","Fresh bread is selling here every morning.","They sell fresh bread here every morning."],"a":"They sell fresh bread here every morning.","hint":"Use a general subject when the exact seller is not named.","exp":"<b>They sell</b> is active and keeps the habitual present meaning."},{"id":"g5eng-syl-022","q":"Which sentence uses <b>must</b> to show a <b>necessity</b>?","opts":["You must sit down, please.","Plants must have water to live.","We must meet again soon.","You must come to school on time."],"a":"Plants must have water to live.","hint":"A necessity is something without which the thing cannot work at all.","exp":"Without water a plant cannot live, so that is a <b>necessity</b>. \"You must come on time\" is an obligation set by a rule, which is the other use of \"must\"."},{"id":"g5e-dep-044","q":"Change to the active: \"The song was sung by Anita.\"","opts":["The song is sung","Anita was singing","The song sang Anita","Anita sang the song"],"a":"Anita sang the song","hint":"Who actually did the action?","exp":"The doer becomes the subject: <b>Anita sang the song.</b>"},{"id":"g5eng-syl-014","q":"What is the difference between <i>She cooked</i> and <i>She was cooking</i>?","opts":["The second shows it never happened","There is no difference at all","The first shows a future plan","The second shows it was still going on"],"a":"The second shows it was still going on","hint":"Ask whether the action is finished or in progress.","exp":"\"She cooked\" is finished; \"She was cooking\" shows the action was <b>still going on</b> at that moment &mdash; which is why it so often ends \"...when the phone rang\"."},{"id":"g5eng-hd-014","q":"Repair both faults:<br><i>\"Neither of the boys were ready, and the list of names have gone missing.\"</i>","opts":["was ready ... has gone missing","was ready ... have gone missing","were ready ... has gone missing","are ready ... have gone missing"],"a":"was ready ... has gone missing","hint":"Cover up \"of the boys\" and \"of names\" and read again.","exp":"The verb answers to <b>neither</b> (one) and to <b>the list</b> (one), not to \"boys\" or \"names\". Cover the middle words and you hear it: \"Neither was ready\", \"the list has gone missing\"."}],"eng-adjectives":[{"id":"g5e-cov-adj-context-2","q":"Which word is the adjective in \"We crossed a narrow bridge\"?","opts":["bridge","We","crossed","narrow"],"a":"narrow","hint":"An adjective describes a noun.","exp":"<b>narrow</b> describes the bridge, so it is the adjective. <i>bridge</i> is the noun and <i>crossed</i> is the verb."},{"id":"g5eng-adj-057","q":"Which sentence uses an adjective of quantity correctly?","opts":["I have a little friends.","I have a few informations.","I have little books.","I have few friends."],"a":"I have few friends.","hint":"\"Few\" (without \"a\") is for countable nouns and suggests \"not enough.\" \"Little\" is for uncountable nouns.","exp":"\"<b>I have few friends</b>\" is correct - \"few\" goes with the countable noun \"friends.\" \"A little\" goes with uncountable nouns. \"Information\" is uncountable (not \"informations\"). \"Little books\" is wrong because \"books\" is countable."},{"id":"g5eng-syl-007","q":"In <i>a round Chinese metal tray</i>, which adjective gives the <b>material</b>?","opts":["metal","Chinese","tray","round"],"a":"metal","hint":"Take each adjective in turn and ask what kind of information it gives.","exp":"\"Round\" is shape, \"Chinese\" is origin and <b>metal</b> is the material. English usually puts them in that order: shape, origin, material."},{"id":"g5eng-hd-020","q":"Repair both faults:<br><i>\"Curepipe is more colder than Port Louis, and it is the most wettest town of the two.\"</i>","opts":["more cold ... the most wet of the two","coldest ... the wetter of the two","colder ... the wetter of the two","colder ... the wettest of the two"],"a":"colder ... the wetter of the two","hint":"How many towns are being compared?","exp":"\"More colder\" doubles up: <b>colder</b> is enough. And with only <b>two</b> towns you compare them, so it is \"the wetter of the two\", never \"the wettest\"."},{"id":"g5eng-syl-008","q":"Which sentence puts the adjectives of shape, origin and material in the usual English order?","opts":["She bought a square Italian leather bag.","She bought a leather square Italian bag.","She bought a leather Italian square bag.","She bought an Italian leather square bag."],"a":"She bought a square Italian leather bag.","hint":"The order runs shape, then origin, then material.","exp":"English puts <b>shape before origin before material</b>: a <i>square</i> (shape) <i>Italian</i> (origin) <i>leather</i> (material) bag."}]},"grade5-french":{"fr-vocabulaire":[{"id":"g5fr-voc-011","q":"Dans « Il a couru à toute vitesse », l’expression « à toute vitesse » veut dire…","opts":["avec prudence","très rapidement","pendant longtemps","sans faire de bruit"],"a":"très rapidement","hint":"Remplace l’expression par un seul mot.","exp":"« À toute vitesse » signifie <b>très rapidement</b>. C’est une locution : les mots pris séparément ne donnent pas le sens."},{"id":"g5fr-voc-001","q":"Nous sommes le 3 mars. Rohan est arrivé l’avant-veille. Quel jour est-il arrivé ?","opts":["le 4 mars","le 5 mars","le 2 mars","le 1er mars"],"a":"le 1er mars","hint":"« L’avant-veille », c’est deux jours avant.","exp":"« La veille » est le jour d’avant et « l’avant-veille » deux jours avant : 3 mars − 2 jours = <b>le 1er mars</b>. Dans l’autre sens on dit « le lendemain » et « le surlendemain »."},{"id":"g5fr-voc-010","q":"L’école est fermée du lundi 5 au vendredi 9 inclus. Combien de jours de classe sont perdus ?","opts":["quatre","cinq","sept","six"],"a":"cinq","hint":"Attention au mot « inclus » : compte les deux jours des extrémités.","exp":"Du lundi au vendredi inclus : lundi, mardi, mercredi, jeudi, vendredi, soit <b>cinq</b> jours. Répondre « quatre » revient à oublier l’un des deux jours des extrémités."},{"id":"g5fr-hd-002","q":"J'ach&egrave;te trois mangues &agrave; 25 roupies chacune et je paie avec un billet de 100 roupies. Combien le marchand me rend-il ?","opts":["soixante-quinze roupies","quinze roupies","vingt-cinq roupies","quatre-vingt-cinq roupies"],"a":"vingt-cinq roupies","hint":"Calculez d'abord le prix des trois mangues, puis retirez-le de 100.","exp":"3 &times; 25 = 75 roupies. 100 - 75 = 25, donc &laquo; vingt-cinq roupies &raquo;. &laquo; Soixante-quinze roupies &raquo; est la somme pay&eacute;e, pas la monnaie rendue."},{"id":"g5fr-voc-019","q":"« Après cette longue marche, j’avais les jambes en coton. » Cela veut dire que…","opts":["mes jambes étaient bien au chaud","mes jambes étaient couvertes de poussière","mes jambes ne me faisaient plus mal","mes jambes étaient faibles et tremblantes"],"a":"mes jambes étaient faibles et tremblantes","hint":"Le coton est mou : que devient une jambe « en coton » ?","exp":"Avoir les jambes <b>en coton</b>, c’est les sentir molles et tremblantes de fatigue. L’expression compare la jambe à une matière sans force ; elle ne parle ni de température ni de saleté."}],"fr-noms":[{"id":"g5fr-nom-028","q":"Quel est le féminin de \"pharmacien\" ?","opts":["pharmacien","pharmacienne","pharmacieure","pharmacière"],"a":"pharmacienne","hint":"-ien → -ienne (double n).","exp":"\"<b>pharmacienne</b>\" - Les noms en -ien font leur féminin en <b>-ienne</b> (double n) : pharmacien → pharmacienne, chirurgien → chirurgienne, musicien → musicienne, technicien → technicienne."},{"id":"g5fr-bal-008","q":"Quel groupe est correct ?","opts":["une bel eau claire","une belle eau claire","un belle eau claire","un bel eau claires"],"a":"une belle eau claire","hint":"« eau » est féminin.","exp":"« Eau » est <b>féminin</b> : une <b>belle</b> eau <b>claire</b>. L’article élidé « l’ » cache souvent le genre, il faut le connaître."},{"id":"g5fr-bal-001","q":"Quel est le pluriel de « un pneu » ?","opts":["des pneaux","des pneux","des pneus","des pneues"],"a":"des pneus","hint":"Presque tous les noms en <b>-eu</b> prennent un x… mais pas celui-ci.","exp":"On écrit <b>des pneus</b>. Les noms en <i>-eu</i> prennent normalement un <b>-x</b> (des jeux, des feux, des cheveux), mais <i>pneu</i> et <i>bleu</i> font exception et prennent un <b>-s</b>."},{"id":"g5fr-hd-008","q":"Quelle phrase est enti&egrave;rement correcte ?","opts":["Cet eau est frais et cette problème est grave.","Cette eau est fraîche et ce problème est grave.","Cette eau est fraîche et cette problème est grave.","Cet eau est fraîche et ce problème est grave."],"a":"Cette eau est fraîche et ce problème est grave.","hint":"&laquo; Eau &raquo; est f&eacute;minin ; &laquo; probl&egrave;me &raquo; est masculin malgr&eacute; son <i>-e</i> final.","exp":"On &eacute;crit <b>cette eau fra&icirc;che</b> (f&eacute;minin) et <b>ce probl&egrave;me</b> (masculin). Beaucoup de noms en <i>-&egrave;me</i> venus du grec sont masculins."},{"id":"g5fr-hd-010","q":"Mettez &agrave; la forme n&eacute;gative : <i>\"Il y a une piscine et des arbres dans la cour.\"</i>","opts":["Il n'y a pas de piscine ni d'arbres dans la cour.","Il n'y a pas une piscine ni des arbres dans la cour.","Il n'y a pas de piscine ni des arbres dans la cour.","Il y a pas de piscine ni d'arbres dans la cour."],"a":"Il n'y a pas de piscine ni d'arbres dans la cour.","hint":"Deux choses changent : la n&eacute;gation compl&egrave;te <i>ne... pas</i>, et les articles.","exp":"Apr&egrave;s une n&eacute;gation, <i>une</i> et <i>des</i> deviennent <b>de</b>, et <b>d'</b> devant une voyelle : &laquo; Il n'y a pas de piscine ni d'arbres dans la cour. &raquo; Le <i>ne</i> ne se supprime pas &agrave; l'&eacute;crit."}],"fr-verbes-present":[{"id":"g5fr-vb-025","q":"Que signifie \"Il faut + infinitif\" ?","opts":["it is necessary to / one must","it is nice to","it is forbidden to","it is possible to"],"a":"it is necessary to / one must","hint":"\"Il faut\" vient du verbe \"falloir\" - il exprime une obligation.","exp":"\"<b>Il faut</b> + infinitif\" = it is necessary to / one must. Exemples : Il faut manger (One must eat). Il faut travailler dur (It is necessary to work hard). \"Il faut\" est impersonnel - il ne change jamais : toujours \"il faut\"."},{"id":"g5fr-vb-015","q":"Verbe réfléchi : \"se lever\" → \"tu\" = ?","opts":["tu vous levez","tu te lèves","tu lèves","tu te lève"],"a":"tu te lèves","hint":"Verbe réfléchi + changement de radical (e → è devant terminaison muette).","exp":"\"<b>tu te lèves</b>\" - se lever : je me lève, tu te lèves, il se lève, nous nous levons, vous vous levez, ils se lèvent. L'accent grave (è) apparaît aux formes singulières et à la 3e personne du pluriel. Même règle : se promener, s'appeler."},{"id":"g5fr-vb-018","q":"Corrigez l'erreur : \"Nous allez à la mer ce week-end.\"","opts":["On allez à la mer ce week-end.","Nous allés à la mer ce week-end.","Nous allons à la mer ce week-end.","Nous aller à la mer ce week-end."],"a":"Nous allons à la mer ce week-end.","hint":"\"Nous\" prend la terminaison -ons. Pour \"aller\" : nous allons.","exp":"\"<b>Nous allons</b>\" - \"aller\" est irrégulier : je vais, tu vas, il va, nous <b>allons</b>, vous allez, ils vont. \"Allez\" est la forme pour \"vous\". C'est une erreur très fréquente d'écrire \"nous allez\" au lieu de \"nous allons\"."},{"id":"g5fr-hd-017","q":"Comment traduit-on : <i>\"They are waiting for the bus.\"</i> ?","opts":["Ils attendes le bus.","Ils attendent pour le bus.","Ils attendent le bus.","Ils sont attendant le bus."],"a":"Ils attendent le bus.","hint":"Le fran&ccedil;ais n'a pas de forme en <i>-ing</i>, et le verbe <i>attendre</i> n'a besoin d'aucune pr&eacute;position.","exp":"On traduit par le pr&eacute;sent simple : &laquo; Ils attendent le bus. &raquo; On ne traduit ni <i>are</i> ni <i>for</i> : &laquo; attendre pour &raquo; est une erreur venue de l'anglais."},{"id":"g5fr-vb-019","q":"Choisissez la phrase CORRECTEMENT conjuguée :","opts":["Mon ami et moi allons au cinéma samedi.","Tu peut venir chez moi demain.","Elle se laves les mains avant de manger.","Les élèves fait leurs devoirs le soir."],"a":"Mon ami et moi allons au cinéma samedi.","hint":"Vérifiez l'accord sujet-verbe dans chaque option.","exp":"\"<b>Mon ami et moi allons</b>\" ✓ - \"mon ami et moi\" = nous → allons. Erreurs : (1) \"Les élèves <b>font</b>\" (pas fait - sujet pluriel), (2) \"Tu <b>peux</b>\" (pas peut - peux pour je/tu), (3) \"Elle <b>se lave</b>\" (pas se laves - pas de -s pour il/elle). L'accord sujet-verbe est l'une des zones les plus testées au PSAC."}]},"grade5-history":{"discovery":[{"id":"g5hg-u2-008","q":"The years 1401 to 1500 form which century?","opts":["The 16th century","The 15th century","The 13th century","The 14th century"],"a":"The 15th century","hint":"Take the first two digits and add one.","exp":"Years <b>1401-1500</b> form the <b>15th century</b>. The rule is to take the hundreds and add one: 1401 is in the 1400s, so it belongs to the 15th century. We ourselves live in the <b>21st century</b>."},{"id":"g5hg-u2-011","q":"The Strait of Magellan, reached in 1520, connects the Atlantic Ocean to which ocean?","opts":["The Pacific Ocean","The Arctic Ocean","The Indian Ocean","The Southern Ocean"],"a":"The Pacific Ocean","hint":"It is the passage around the southern tip of South America.","exp":"Ferdinand Magellan reached the strait in <b>1520</b>. The passage connects the <b>Atlantic</b> to the <b>Pacific</b> Ocean, and it was later named the <b>Strait of Magellan</b> after him."},{"id":"g5hg-u2-029","q":"Besides food, water and rest, what made Mauritius a good stop-over for Dutch sailors?","opts":["A deep harbour already built by the Arabs","A short route straight back to Holland","A good climate and freedom from serious diseases","A large town with a market and shops"],"a":"A good climate and freedom from serious diseases","hint":"Many tropical ports of the day made sailors ill.","exp":"Mauritius offered food, fresh water, rest, <b>a good climate and freedom from serious diseases</b>. On a voyage of many months, a healthy stop was worth as much as a full water barrel."},{"id":"g5hg-hd-008","q":"The Dutch landed in 1598 but did not settle until 1638 — a gap of forty years.<br>What does that gap most likely show?","opts":["The island was occupied by the Portuguese","They could not find the island again","Their ships were too small to carry settlers","Settling was costly and needed a reason"],"a":"Settling was costly and needed a reason","hint":"Visiting an island and living on it demand very different resources.","exp":"A landing costs one stop; a settlement costs people, supplies and years. The forty-year gap shows the Dutch <b>weighed the cost</b> — and only committed once ebony and a strategic harbour made it worthwhile."},{"id":"g5hg-hd-011","q":"The Dutch abandoned Mauritius twice, yet the French settled successfully soon afterwards.<br>Which difference best explains the outcome?","opts":["The French treated it as a colony, not a supply stop","The Dutch had exhausted all the island's water","The French arrived with far more ships","The French found the island uninhabited"],"a":"The French treated it as a colony, not a supply stop","hint":"What were the Dutch using the island FOR?","exp":"The Dutch used the island as a <b>waypoint</b> and left when it stopped paying. The French invested in a capital, a port and plantations — building a place people would stay. Both found it uninhabited, so that cannot be the difference."}],"settlement":[{"id":"g5hg-cov-sett-french-6","q":"Why did the French want a strong harbour?","opts":["to attract more settlers","to store sugar and spices","for trade and naval ships","to collect taxes from ships"],"a":"for trade and naval ships","hint":"Think about how the French used the island, who led them, and what they built.","exp":"A strong harbour served <b>trade and naval ships</b> at once — merchants, and warships guarding the sea route."},{"id":"g5hg-u3-053","q":"Which island did the British capture before entering the bay at Grand Port in 1810?","opts":["&Icirc;le aux Aigrettes","&Icirc;le de la Passe","&Icirc;le Cocos","&Icirc;le aux Cerfs"],"a":"&Icirc;le de la Passe","hint":"Its name says what it guards — the pass into the bay.","exp":"The British captured <b>&Icirc;le de la Passe</b>, which guards the entrance, and entered the bay. Their warships then became trapped in shallow sandy areas and the French won the battle."},{"id":"g5hg-u3-028","q":"Where did the British land in November 1810 for their final attack?","opts":["Mahebourg","Grand Port","Cap Malheureux","Port Louis"],"a":"Cap Malheureux","hint":"It is at the very northern tip of Mauritius.","exp":"In <b>November 1810</b> the British prepared again in Rodrigues and landed at <b>Cap Malheureux</b> with more warships, weapons, British soldiers and Indian soldiers, then marched towards Port Louis."},{"id":"g5hg-hd-023","q":"A visitor in 1700 finds cleared forest, escaped deer and pigs, and no dodos.<br>Which single cause links all three observations?","opts":["Human settlement of the island","Volcanic activity in the interior","A change in the island's climate","A severe cyclone some years earlier"],"a":"Human settlement of the island","hint":"Look for the one cause that explains clearing, new animals AND a disappearance.","exp":"Settlers felled forest, released livestock that went wild, and hunted or displaced the dodo. A cyclone or an eruption could damage forest but could not <b>introduce deer</b> — only people do that."},{"id":"g5hg-hd-016","q":"At the Battle of Grand Port in 1810 the French won, yet Britain held the island by the end of that same year.<br>How can both be true?","opts":["The battle took place after the surrender","The French victory was recorded incorrectly","Britain landed elsewhere in far greater force","The French handed the island over willingly"],"a":"Britain landed elsewhere in far greater force","hint":"Winning one battle is not the same as winning a campaign.","exp":"Grand Port was a real French naval victory, but Britain returned in strength and landed in the north, at Cap Malheureux. <b>A battle is not a war</b> — this is why historians distinguish the two."}],"trade-agri":[{"id":"g5hg-ta-007","q":"The SSR Botanical Garden at Pamplemousses is one of the oldest botanical gardens in the southern hemisphere. Which French governor first developed it for growing spices?","opts":["Mahé de Labourdonnais","Robert Farquhar","Charles Decaen","Pierre Poivre"],"a":"Pierre Poivre","hint":"He needed a place to cultivate the spice plants he had brought from the East Indies.","exp":"<b>Pierre Poivre</b> developed the Pamplemousses gardens as a place to grow and study the spice plants he introduced from the East Indies. The garden is now a national heritage site."},{"id":"g5hg-u3-017","q":"What did Pierre Poivre distribute to the settlers from Pamplemousses?","opts":["Maps of the Indian Ocean routes","Seeds and seedlings of fruits and spices","Money borrowed from the French king","Ox carts and farming tools"],"a":"Seeds and seedlings of fruits and spices","hint":"A botanical garden is a place where plants are raised.","exp":"<b>Pierre Poivre</b> created the <b>Botanical Garden of Pamplemousses</b> and introduced fruits, medicinal plants and valuable spices such as cloves and nutmeg. <b>Seeds and seedlings were distributed to settlers</b>, so the whole colony could grow them."},{"id":"g5hg-hd-034","q":"Why did the opening of the Suez Canal in 1869 hurt Port Louis?","opts":["Cyclones increased after it opened","Ships to India no longer passed the island","The canal flooded the harbour","Sugar could no longer be exported"],"a":"Ships to India no longer passed the island","hint":"The island's value was its position on a route. What happened to the route?","exp":"The canal let ships reach India through the Mediterranean instead of round the Cape. Mauritius was suddenly <b>off the main road</b> — a reminder that a place's advantages can be taken away by events far off."},{"id":"g5hg-hd-033","q":"Mauritius today earns from tourism, textiles, finance and sugar rather than sugar alone.<br>What is the main advantage?","opts":["Sugar has become far more profitable","Every industry now earns exactly the same","Fewer workers are needed altogether","A shock to one industry no longer sinks the country"],"a":"A shock to one industry no longer sinks the country","hint":"This is the cure for the danger of depending on one crop.","exp":"Spreading income across several industries means a cyclone, or a fall in the sugar price, hurts <b>part</b> of the economy rather than all of it. That is what diversification is for."},{"id":"g5hg-hd-025","q":"Sugar cane withstands strong wind better than most crops, and Mauritius lies in a cyclone belt.<br>How are those two facts connected?","opts":["Cane survives storms that would ruin other crops","Cane grows faster because of cyclones","Cane protects the island from cyclones","Cyclones are needed to water the cane"],"a":"Cane survives storms that would ruin other crops","hint":"Ask what a farmer in a cyclone zone needs from a crop.","exp":"A flattened cane field usually recovers; an orchard does not. In a cyclone belt, <b>survivability outweighs yield</b> — one reason cane dominated Mauritian farming for so long."}]},"grade5-maths":{"numeration":[{"id":"CH_NUM06","q":"Which number, when rounded to the nearest million, gives 5,000,000?","opts":["4,456,789","5,678,432","5,500,000","4,500,001"],"a":"4,500,001","hint":"Any number from 4,500,000 to 5,499,999 rounds to 5 million.","exp":"4,500,001 is in the range [4,500,000–5,499,999] so it rounds to 5,000,000."},{"id":"CH_NUM12","q":"Write MMXXVI as a number.","opts":["2024","2016","2216","2026"],"a":"2026","hint":"MM=2000, XX=20, VI=6","exp":"MM(2000)+XX(20)+VI(6) = 2,026."},{"id":"CH_NUM10","q":"What is MCDXLIV in Hindu-Arabic numerals?","opts":["1444","1404","1544","1344"],"a":"1444","hint":"M=1000, CD=400, XL=40, IV=4","exp":"M(1000)+CD(400)+XL(40)+IV(4) = 1,444."},{"id":"CH_NUM02","q":"Round 2,847,392 to the nearest ten thousand.","opts":["2,847,000","2,840,000","2,850,000","2,900,000"],"a":"2,850,000","hint":"Look at the thousands digit (7) - is it ≥ 5?","exp":"Thousands digit is 7 ≥ 5, so ten-thousands rounds up: 2,850,000."},{"id":"CH_NUM16","q":"A school fundraiser collects 2,847,395 centimes. Rounded to the nearest ten thousand it is A. Rounded to the nearest hundred thousand it is B. What is A − B?","opts":["100,000","60,000","50,000","40,000"],"a":"50,000","hint":"A = 2,850,000; B = 2,800,000","exp":"2,847,395 → nearest ten-thousand = 2,850,000 (A). Nearest hundred-thousand = 2,800,000 (B). A−B = 50,000."}],"four_ops":[{"id":"F14","q":"What is the <b>remainder</b> when 7,654 is divided by 9?","opts":["1","2","6","4"],"a":"4","hint":"Find the largest multiple of 9 below 7,654 and see what is left over. Adding the digits together also helps.","exp":"9 × 850 = 7,650. 7,654 − 7,650 = <b>4</b>. Remainder = 4."},{"id":"CH_FOP18","q":"What is the remainder when 5,003 is divided by 17?","opts":["7","2","9","5"],"a":"5","hint":"17 × 294 = 4,998. Then 5,003 − 4,998 = ?","exp":"17 × 294 = 4,998, and 17 × 295 = 5,015 which is already past 5,003. So 5,003 ÷ 17 = 294 remainder <b>5</b>."},{"id":"CH_FOP04","q":"What is 36 × 25?","opts":["960","800","900","850"],"a":"900","hint":"36 × 25 = 36 × 100 ÷ 4","exp":"36 × 25 = 36 × 100 ÷ 4 = 3600 ÷ 4 = 900."},{"id":"g5m-syl-010","q":"A shop divides 24 000 seeds equally into packets of 1 000, then divides one packet into 10 small bags. How many seeds are in each small bag?","opts":["240","100","1 000","24"],"a":"100","hint":"Do the two divisions one after the other.","exp":"24 000 &divide; 1 000 = 24 packets of 1 000 seeds. Then 1 000 &divide; 10 = <b>100</b> seeds in each small bag."},{"id":"CH_FOP14","q":"Priya earns Rs 5,400 a month. She spends ⅓ on rent, ¼ on food, and saves the rest. How much does she save each month?","opts":["Rs 3,050","Rs 2,250","Rs 1,800","Rs 1,350"],"a":"Rs 2,250","hint":"Rent = 5400÷3. Food = 5400÷4. Save = 5400 − rent − food.","exp":"Rent=1800, Food=1350. Spent=3150. Saved=5400−3150=Rs 2,250."}],"square_nums":[{"id":"S03","q":"Which of these is a <b>perfect square number</b>?","opts":["45","36","50","72"],"a":"36","hint":"A perfect square is a whole number multiplied by itself. Work through 4×4, 5×5, 6×6, 7×7 and compare with the options.","exp":"6 × 6 = 36, so <b>36</b> is a perfect square. (50, 72, 45 are not perfect squares.)"},{"id":"CH_SQN12","q":"How many square numbers are there between 50 and 150?","opts":["6","5","4","3"],"a":"5","hint":"List the squares in order and keep the ones bigger than 50 and smaller than 150.","exp":"8²=64, 9²=81, 10²=100, 11²=121, 12²=144 — <b>5</b> square numbers lie between 50 and 150. 7²=49 is too small and 13²=169 is too big."},{"id":"CH_SQN09","q":"The difference between two consecutive square numbers is 25. What is the larger square number?","opts":["156","196","169","144"],"a":"169","hint":"If n² − (n−1)² = 25, then 2n − 1 = 25, so n = 13.","exp":"(n)² − (n−1)² = 2n − 1 = 25 → n = 13. 13² = 169."},{"id":"CH_SQN20","q":"A square picture frame has an outer side of 30 cm and an inner side of 22 cm. What is the area of the frame itself?","opts":["900 cm²","416 cm²","436 cm²","484 cm²"],"a":"416 cm²","hint":"Outer area = 30². Inner area = 22². Frame = outer − inner.","exp":"30²=900. 22²=484. Frame=900−484=416 cm²."},{"id":"CH_SQN14","q":"Dev saves money each week. Week 1: Rs 1, Week 2: Rs 4, Week 3: Rs 9, Week 4: Rs 16. If this pattern continues, how much does he save in Week 8?","opts":["Rs 56","Rs 64","Rs 49","Rs 81"],"a":"Rs 64","hint":"Each week he saves n² rupees.","exp":"Week 8: 8² = Rs 64."}]},"grade5-science":{"plants":[{"id":"g5sci-pl-008","q":"Which measure helps to PREVENT soil erosion on slopes?","opts":["Using heavy machinery on slopes","Planting trees and plants on slopes","Leaving land bare after harvesting","Cutting down all trees on the slope"],"a":"Planting trees and plants on slopes","hint":"Plant roots hold the soil in place.","exp":"Planting trees and plants on slopes prevents soil erosion because the roots bind the soil together and prevent it from being washed or blown away."},{"id":"g5sc-dep-095","q":"Give one difference between photosynthesis and respiration.","opts":["One makes food, one uses it","Both take place at night","Both give out oxygen","Neither needs any water"],"a":"One makes food, one uses it","hint":"Think about which gas each one takes in.","exp":"Photosynthesis makes food and takes in carbon dioxide; respiration uses food and takes in oxygen. <b>One makes food, the other uses it.</b>"},{"id":"g5sci-syl-004","q":"Mosses and ferns are grouped together. Why?","opts":["Both of them live in water","Both of them grow very tall","Neither of them produces flowers","Neither of them has leaves"],"a":"Neither of them produces flowers","hint":"Think about what is missing from both of them.","exp":"Mosses and ferns are both <b>non-flowering</b> plants: they never produce flowers, and they spread by spores instead of seeds."},{"id":"g5sci-syl-007","q":"A pupil classifies plants by the colour of their pots. Why is that a poor way to group them?","opts":["Pots are all the same size","Colours are hard to see","The pot is not part of the plant","Plants do not grow in pots"],"a":"The pot is not part of the plant","hint":"A useful grouping uses something that belongs to the living thing itself.","exp":"A pot can be changed tomorrow; it tells you nothing about the plant. Classification must use the <b>parts of the plant itself</b> &mdash; leaves, stem, roots, flowers."},{"id":"g5sci-syl-011","q":"A gardener waters her plants well but they still grow poorly in a shady, cold corner. What is missing?","opts":["Leaves and stems","Water and air","Light and warmth","Seeds and roots"],"a":"Light and warmth","hint":"List the conditions a plant needs, then tick off the ones it already has.","exp":"The plants have water, but a shady cold corner leaves them short of <b>light and warmth</b>. A plant needs every condition, not just one."}],"animals":[{"id":"g5sc-pp19-009","q":"Which bird is <strong>endemic</strong> to the island of Mauritius and is known as the national bird?","opts":["Sparrow","Dove","Kestrel","Crow"],"a":"Kestrel","hint":"Mauritius chose an endemic raptor as its national symbol - which of these is a bird of prey?","exp":"The Mauritius Kestrel (Falco punctatus) is endemic to Mauritius and is the national bird. It was saved from extinction through a successful breeding programme."},{"id":"g5sc-pp21-011","q":"What type of bird is the Mauritius Kestrel?","opts":["Endangered","Endemic","Exotic","Extinct"],"a":"Endemic","hint":"This word describes a species found naturally in one specific place and nowhere else.","exp":"The Mauritius Kestrel (Falco punctatus) is endemic to Mauritius - it is found in the wild only on the island of Mauritius. It was saved from extinction through conservation."},{"id":"g5sci-an-017","q":"A dolphin breathes air using lungs, but it lives in the sea. Which group of animals does a dolphin belong to?","opts":["Amphibian","Mammal","Reptile","Fish"],"a":"Mammal","hint":"If it breathes air, is warm-blooded and feeds its young on milk, it is in this group.","exp":"The dolphin is a <b>mammal</b>. Although it lives in the sea and looks like a fish, it breathes air through a blowhole (it has lungs, not gills), is warm-blooded, gives birth to live young, and nurses them with milk - all characteristics of mammals."},{"id":"g5sci-an-001","q":"A species is described as <b>endemic</b> to Mauritius. What does this tell you about it?","opts":["It was brought to Mauritius by early settlers","It lives on several islands in the region","It is found naturally nowhere else in the world","It is in danger of dying out completely"],"a":"It is found naturally nowhere else in the world","hint":"The word says where the species belongs, not how rare it is.","exp":"<b>Endemic</b> means found naturally in one place and nowhere else on Earth. It is not the same as endangered - a species can be endemic and still be common - and it is the opposite of introduced."},{"id":"g5sci-an-019","q":"A student argues: \"Animals should be taken from nature reserves and kept in zoos, because zoos are safer.\" Give the BEST argument AGAINST this view.","opts":["Zoos are too expensive for families to visit","Zoos always mistreat the animals they keep","Wild animals cannot survive in zoos at all","In zoos, animals cannot live and behave naturally"],"a":"In zoos, animals cannot live and behave naturally","hint":"What is the purpose of a nature reserve compared to a zoo?","exp":"Nature reserves are designed to allow animals to live naturally in their own habitat, hunt their own food and reproduce naturally - this is the goal of conservation. Moving animals to zoos removes them from their natural habitat, can reduce their quality of life, and depletes the very wild population that conservation programmes aim to grow. Captive breeding in zoos can play a supplementary role, but nature reserves in the wild are considered more sustainable."}],"energy":[{"id":"g5sc-dep-036","q":"Give one way of saving electricity at home.","opts":["Keep the television on","Switch off unused lights","Boil a full kettle daily","Leave the fridge door open"],"a":"Switch off unused lights","hint":"Stop using what nobody needs.","exp":"<b>Switching off unused lights</b> stops electricity being wasted in empty rooms."},{"id":"g5sc-pp20-003","q":"Why do <strong>more families in Mauritius</strong> choose solar water heaters than electric ones?","opts":["Mauritius has plenty of free, renewable sunshine.","Solar heaters heat the water much faster.","Electric heaters are much more expensive to buy.","Solar heaters work much better at night."],"a":"Mauritius has plenty of free, renewable sunshine.","hint":"Mauritius is a tropical island near the equator - what does it have plenty of?","exp":"Mauritius is close to the equator and receives lots of sunlight all year. Solar energy is free and renewable, making solar water heaters cheaper to run than electric ones."},{"id":"g5sc-dep-015","q":"What does \"renewable\" mean when describing energy?","opts":["It gives out no heat","It is used only at night","It is very expensive","It is replaced naturally"],"a":"It is replaced naturally","hint":"Nature keeps supplying it.","exp":"A renewable source <b>is replaced naturally</b>, so using it today does not reduce what is there tomorrow."},{"id":"g5sc-dep-039","q":"Which change would save the most energy in a classroom?","opts":["Opening windows instead of using fans","Leaving computers on all night","Turning on every light at noon","Closing all the curtains at noon"],"a":"Opening windows instead of using fans","hint":"Use what nature provides.","exp":"<b>Opening windows instead of using fans</b> cools the room with no electricity at all."},{"id":"g5sc-dep-012","q":"Which source of energy would work best on a roof in a sunny place?","opts":["Solar panels","A coal furnace","A water turbine","A petrol engine"],"a":"Solar panels","hint":"Use what is freely available there.","exp":"<b>Solar panels</b> turn sunlight into electricity, and a sunny roof receives sunlight all day."}]}}};
